<article class="env-remark" id="04R4">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04R4">Remark <span data-tag="04R4">35.32.8</span>.</a> At this point we have three possible definitions of what it means for a property $\mathcal{P}$ of morphisms to be “étale local on the source and target”: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="(ST)"><p>
$\mathcal{P}$ is étale local on the source and $\mathcal{P}$ is étale local on the target, 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(DM)"><p>
(the definition in the paper <span class="cite">[<span class="postnote">Page 100</span>, <a href="/bibliography/DM">DM</a>]</span> by Deligne and Mumford) for every diagram 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ U \ar[d]_ a \ar[r]_ h &  V \ar[d]^ b \\  X \ar[r]^ f &  Y }  \]
</div>
<p>
 with surjective étale vertical arrows we have $\mathcal{P}(h) \Leftrightarrow \mathcal{P}(f)$, and 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(SP)"><p>
$\mathcal{P}$ is étale local on the source-and-target. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> In this section we have seen that (SP) $\Rightarrow $ (DM) $\Rightarrow $ (ST). The Examples <a href="/tag/04QX" data-tag="04QX">35.32.1</a> and <a href="/tag/04QY" data-tag="04QY">35.32.2</a> show that neither implication can be reversed. Finally, Lemma <a href="/tag/04R2" data-tag="04R2">35.32.6</a> shows that the difference disappears when looking at properties of morphisms which are stable under postcomposing with open immersions, which in practice will always be the case. </p>
</article>